a number called gen_lists is 0.

a number called gen_maps is 0.

(Lists: declaration, append, and element access - in range and
 deliberately out of it, same claim under test as buffers.)

To 'gen leaf list inrange' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_lists to gen_lists add 1.
    a number called 'elem one' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'elem two' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called decl is "a list called l{gen_lists} is [{'elem one'}, {'elem two'}]".
    a number called appended is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called 'append line' is "append {appended} to l{gen_lists}".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print element 1 of l{gen_lists}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'append line', 'read line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf list oob' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_lists to gen_lists add 1.
    a number called 'elem one' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'elem two' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called decl is "a list called l{gen_lists} is [{'elem one'}, {'elem two'}]".
    a number called oob is 'rng below' of 500 add 10.
    a text called 'read line' is "Print element {oob} of l{gen_lists}".
    a text called handler is "On error print \"list read out of range\"".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'read line', handler].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Maps: literal creation with a text-valued key and a number-valued key,
 string-key read, `Set map's "key" to ...`, `'s length`, and a `keys`
 iteration - heap growth and hashing (finding 05). The oob variant reads
 a key that was never inserted; the language's own claim is that a
 missing key sets the error flag and returns 0 rather than crashing, so
 this is the same on-trial pattern as the buffer/list oob leaves. Each
 map gets a freshly numbered iteration variable (k{N}) for the same
 fresh-name reason the grid loop variables do.)

To 'gen leaf map inrange' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_maps to gen_maps add 1.
    a number called 'count value' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a number called 'count value two' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called decl is "a map called m{gen_maps} is {{\"label\": \"tag{gen_maps}\", \"count\": {'count value'}}}".
    a text called 'read label' is "Print m{gen_maps}'s \"label\"".
    a text called 'write count' is "Set m{gen_maps}'s \"count\" to {'count value two'}".
    a text called 'read count' is "Print m{gen_maps}'s \"count\"".
    a text called 'read length' is "Print m{gen_maps}'s length".
    a text called 'iterate keys' is "For each k{gen_maps} in m{gen_maps}'s keys, Print k{gen_maps}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'read label', 'write count', 'read count', 'read length', 'iterate keys'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

To 'gen leaf map oob' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_maps to gen_maps add 1.
    a number called 'count value' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called decl is "a map called m{gen_maps} is {{\"label\": \"tag{gen_maps}\", \"count\": {'count value'}}}".
    a text called 'read line' is "Print m{gen_maps}'s \"missing\"".
    a text called handler is "On error print \"map key missing\"".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'read line', handler].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Mixed-type lists: a number, a text, and a float literal in one list -
 per-slot runtime type tag machinery, never author-declared. Iterating
 and printing exercises the tag dispatch on read (grep-proven gap, same
 standard as the four findings). Each list gets its own iteration
 variable (w{N}) for the same fresh-name reason maps and the grid do.)

To 'gen leaf list mixed' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_lists to gen_lists add 1.
    a number called 'number element' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'float whole' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a number called 'float frac' is 'rng below' of 100.
    a text called decl is "a list called l{gen_lists} is [{'number element'}, \"mixed{gen_lists}\", {'float whole'}.{'float frac'}]".
    a text called 'iterate line' is "For each w{gen_lists} in l{gen_lists}, Print w{gen_lists}".
    a list called 'the lines' is [decl, 'iterate line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(--- Dynamic values: the `value` type --------------------------------

 Ledger docs/ledger/values.md; LANGUAGE.md 2436-2578. Rows VAL-03,
 VAL-05, VAL-06, VAL-07, VAL-09, VAL-10, VAL-11 and VAL-13.

 Every leaf below ASSERTS. The generator picked the runtime tag and the
 payload, so it knows what the program must do; where it does not, the
 program prints one line `ASSERT VAL-NN: expected <x> got <y>` and exits
 95, which the runner reports as a wrong-value finding.

 All eight are TOP-LEVEL ONLY - drawn at depth 3 and nowhere else, and
 deliberately absent from 'gen any leaf'. Two properties of the shapes
 force that, neither of them special to this file:

 - VAL-03 and VAL-05 emit a `To ...` definition, which is a top-level
   construct.
 - every assertion is `If ... then, Print "...", Exit 95.`, and that
   period closes the If (LANGUAGE.md rule 1). As the last thing in a
   nested body it would leave the enclosing block open - the same trap
   documented at length on 'gen statement's avoid-onerror remap. The
   leaves are safe wherever a following plain statement closes the
   block, which at the true top level is guaranteed and inside a body
   is not.

 The `got` half of an assertion message reads `{<name>'s type}`, not
 `{<name>}`, wherever the value's tag is still dynamic and not a number:
 interpolating a dynamically-tagged value into a format string prints
 its raw payload as an integer rather than dispatching on the tag - a
 text comes out as a pointer, a float as its bit pattern. Hand-reduced;
 recorded as a discrepancy for the master, NOT worked around anywhere
 the assertion's own CONDITION depends on it.)

(The generated programs are read by scripts/invariants and by people, so
 the names in them are held to vox/docs/STYLE.md exactly as the
 generator's own are: a name is the thing's true name, never y2 or r. A
 leaf spends its names out of these vocabularies - nouns for the data,
 verbs for the functions, and a distinguishing word for the second lap
 round a vocabulary. Both name vocabularies are a PRIME number of words
 long, which is what lets the cycle below visit every one of them before
 repeating; adding or removing a word means picking a new prime.

 The nonsense list is different in kind: it is payload, not names -
 sentences chosen because no reading of them parses as a number.)

a list called 'the value nouns' is ["the parcel", "whatever", "the answer", "payload", "what arrived", "the cargo", "cargo", "the reading", "the tally", "the token", "token", "the slot", "the label", "what came back", "the numeral", "numeral", "the guess", "guess", "the offering", "offering", "the receipt", "receipt", "the sample", "sample", "the shipment", "shipment", "whatever turned up", "the contents", "contents", "the wrapper", "wrapper", "the ticket", "ticket", "the bundle", "bundle", "the remainder", "the message", "the finding", "the delivery", "the note", "the marker"].

a list called 'the value verbs' is ["sort out", "hand back", "describe", "classify", "give back", "echo", "pass through", "inspect", "work out", "relay", "send onward", "identify", "forward", "carry over", "examine", "take a look", "announce", "read it out", "label", "make sense of", "report", "hand along", "recognise", "look it over", "deliver", "name the tag", "make out", "puzzle out", "hand over"].

a list called 'the distinguishing words' is ["again", "once more", "in turn", "later", "as well", "beside it", "in reserve", "after that"].

a list called 'the value nonsense' is ["not a numeral", "wide of the mark", "no digits here", "quite unparseable", "nothing like a number", "words only", "a sentence instead", "plainly not numeric"].

(Names are spent out of the vocabulary on a CYCLE rather than drawn and
 retried: a random starting point and a random stride per program, both
 vocabularies being a prime number of words long, so the stride is
 coprime with the length and the walk visits every word before it
 repeats. Two names in one program are distinct by construction, with no
 record of what has been spent to search - which matters, because a
 budget-300 program spends names in the hundreds and a linear search
 would make that quadratic.

 Past one full lap the word takes a distinguishing suffix, so lap two of
 `the parcel` is `the parcel again`.)

a number called gen_nouns_spent is 0.

a number called gen_noun_offset is 0.

a number called gen_noun_stride is 1.

a number called gen_verbs_spent is 0.

a number called gen_verb_offset is 0.

a number called gen_verb_stride is 1.

a boolean called gen_value_word_used is false.

(For payload CONTENT - a word inside a text literal, a map key, a
 sentence that will not parse as a number. Nothing here has to be
 unique, so it is a plain draw, unlike the name cycle above.)

To 'gen word from' with a list called vocabulary.
    a number called 'the pick' is 'rng below' of vocabulary's length.
    a number called 'the slot' is 'the pick' add 1.
    a text called 'the word' is element 'the slot' of vocabulary.
    Return a text, 'the word'.

To 'gen reset value names'.
    Set gen_nouns_spent to 0.
    Set gen_verbs_spent to 0.
    Set gen_value_word_used to false.
    a number called 'how many nouns' is 'the value nouns's length.
    Set gen_noun_offset to 'rng below' of 'how many nouns'.
    a number called 'the noun stride room' is 'how many nouns' minus 1.
    a number called 'a noun stride' is 'rng below' of 'the noun stride room'.
    Set gen_noun_stride to 'a noun stride' add 1.
    a number called 'how many verbs' is 'the value verbs's length.
    Set gen_verb_offset to 'rng below' of 'how many verbs'.
    a number called 'the verb stride room' is 'how many verbs' minus 1.
    a number called 'a verb stride' is 'rng below' of 'the verb stride room'.
    Set gen_verb_stride to 'a verb stride' add 1.

To 'gen cycled word' with a list called vocabulary and a number called spent and a number called offset and a number called stride.
    a number called 'how many words' is vocabulary's length.
    a number called 'the step' is spent multiply stride.
    a number called 'the raw slot' is 'the step' add offset.
    a number called 'the slot' is 'the raw slot' modulo 'how many words'.
    a number called 'the position' is 'the slot' add 1.
    a text called 'the word' is element 'the position' of vocabulary.
    a number called 'the lap' is spent divide 'how many words'.
    If 'the lap' is 0 then,
        Return a text, 'the word'.
    a number called 'how many distinguishing words' is 'the distinguishing words's length.
    a number called 'the lap before this one' is 'the lap' minus 1.
    a number called 'the suffix slot' is 'the lap before this one' modulo 'how many distinguishing words'.
    a number called 'the suffix position' is 'the suffix slot' add 1.
    a text called 'a distinguishing word' is element 'the suffix position' of 'the distinguishing words'.
    a text called 'a longer name' is "{'the word'} {'a distinguishing word'}".
    Return a text, 'a longer name'.

To 'gen fresh noun'.
    Set gen_nouns_spent to gen_nouns_spent add 1.
    a text called 'the phrase' is 'gen cycled word' of 'the value nouns' and gen_nouns_spent and gen_noun_offset and gen_noun_stride.
    Return a text, 'the phrase'.

To 'gen fresh verb'.
    Set gen_verbs_spent to gen_verbs_spent add 1.
    a text called 'the phrase' is 'gen cycled word' of 'the value verbs' and gen_verbs_spent and gen_verb_offset and gen_verb_stride.
    Return a text, 'the phrase'.

(A space is what decides whether a name must be written quoted, so the
 phrase is scanned for one rather than guessed at from the vocabulary it
 came out of - a distinguishing word turns a bare noun into a phrase.)

To 'phrase has a space' with a text called phrase.
    a buffer called letters is "{phrase}".
    a number called position is 1.
    a boolean called 'a space appears' is false.
    a number called 'how long' is letters's length.
    While position is less than or equal to 'how long',
        a number called 'the character code' is byte position of letters,
        If 'the character code' is 32 then,
            Set 'a space appears' to true.
        Set position to position add 1.
    Return a boolean, 'a space appears'.

(A name is carried around as its bare phrase and written out in one of
 two forms, because Vox needs both and they are not the same string: a
 phrase with a space in it must be quoted, and the possessive puts its
 `'s` INSIDE the closing quote (`'the contents's type`, never
 `'the contents''s type`).

 A single word is never quoted, even though `a number called 'wrapper'`
 is a perfectly legal declaration. Quoting one is legal everywhere the
 generator needs it EXCEPT inside a format string, where `{'wrapper'}`
 is rejected as an unknown variable while `{'the parcel'}` - the same
 quoting, two words - resolves. Hand-reduced; recorded as a discrepancy
 for the master. The generator writes the form that works rather than
 encoding a reading of which one ought to.)

To 'gen written name' with a text called phrase.
    a boolean called 'must be quoted' is 'phrase has a space' of phrase.
    If 'must be quoted' then,
        a text called 'the quoted name' is "'{phrase}'",
        Return a text, 'the quoted name'.
    Return a text, phrase.

To 'gen possessive name' with a text called phrase.
    a boolean called 'must be quoted' is 'phrase has a space' of phrase.
    If 'must be quoted' then,
        a text called 'the quoted possessive' is "'{phrase}'s",
        Return a text, 'the quoted possessive'.
    a text called 'the plain possessive' is "{phrase}'s".
    Return a text, 'the plain possessive'.

(VAL-06 in passing: `value` is not reserved (LANGUAGE.md:2479), so it is
 a legal parameter name and the name-pool must not blacklist it. A
 parameter is local to its own function, so this competes with nothing -
 hand-verified that a function taking `a value called value` and a
 top-level variable named `value` coexist in one program. The top-level
 declaration is the one that has to be unique, and 'gen leaf value name'
 owns that.)

To 'gen value parameter name'.
    a number called 'the naming choice' is 'rng below' of 5.
    If 'the naming choice' is 0 then,
        Return a text, "value".
    a text called 'an ordinary name' is 'gen fresh noun'.
    Return a text, 'an ordinary name'.

(The seven runtime tags a `value` can carry, as LANGUAGE.md:2549 lists
 them: 0 number, 1 text, 2 float, 3 boolean, 4 list, 5 map, 6 nothing.
 `nothing` is the odd one out - there is no `is a nothing` predicate
 (VAL-25), so it matches no branch and lands in Otherwise.)

To 'gen tag predicate' with a number called tag.
    If tag is 0 then,
        Return a text, "a number".
    If tag is 1 then,
        Return a text, "a text".
    If tag is 2 then,
        (LANGUAGE.md:2509 gives `float` and `decimal` as the same target,
         so neither spelling may be the one this generator always picks.)
        a number called 'the spelling' is 'rng below' of 2,
        If 'the spelling' is 0 then,
            Return a text, "a decimal".
        Return a text, "a float".
    If tag is 3 then,
        Return a text, "a boolean".
    If tag is 4 then,
        Return a text, "a list".
    Return a text, "a map".

To 'gen tag name' with a number called tag.
    If tag is 0 then,
        Return a text, "a number".
    If tag is 1 then,
        Return a text, "a text".
    If tag is 2 then,
        Return a text, "a float".
    If tag is 3 then,
        Return a text, "a boolean".
    If tag is 4 then,
        Return a text, "a list".
    If tag is 5 then,
        Return a text, "a map".
    Return a text, "nothing".

(A payload, an expression that writes it into a program, and a form
 that is safe to drop inside an assertion message. They are three
 functions rather than one because a text payload needs its quotes three
 different ways: bare when the generator reasons about it, `"..."` in
 the program, and `\\"..."` inside the double-quoted message.)

(The same seven words without their article, for the message positions
 where "expected no a number argument" would be the alternative.)

To 'gen bare tag name' with a number called tag.
    If tag is 0 then,
        Return a text, "number".
    If tag is 1 then,
        Return a text, "text".
    If tag is 2 then,
        Return a text, "float".
    If tag is 3 then,
        Return a text, "boolean".
    If tag is 4 then,
        Return a text, "list".
    If tag is 5 then,
        Return a text, "map".
    Return a text, "nothing".

(A text payload. `"<word> <0-999>"` was every text this generator made,
 which is a rule nobody wrote: the manual says nothing about what a text
 in a `value` looks like. Empty, one word, several words, and a word
 with a trailing number are all drawn.)

To 'gen text payload'.
    a number called 'the shape' is 'rng below' of 5.
    If 'the shape' is 0 then,
        Return a text, "".
    a text called 'a phrase' is 'gen word from' of 'the value nouns'.
    If 'the shape' is 1 then,
        Return a text, 'a phrase'.
    If 'the shape' is 2 then,
        a text called 'another phrase' is 'gen word from' of 'the value nouns',
        a text called 'two phrases' is "{'a phrase'} {'another phrase'}",
        Return a text, 'two phrases'.
    If 'the shape' is 3 then,
        a text called 'a third phrase' is 'gen word from' of 'the value nouns',
        a text called 'a fourth phrase' is 'gen word from' of 'the value nouns',
        a text called 'several phrases' is "{'a phrase'} {'a third phrase'} {'a fourth phrase'}",
        Return a text, 'several phrases'.
    a number called 'a count' is 'rng below' of 1000.
    a text called 'a phrase and a number' is "{'a phrase'} {'a count'}".
    Return a text, 'a phrase and a number'.

(One slot of an aggregate: a scalar of a randomly drawn tag. `nothing`
 is deliberately NOT drawn here - a `nothing` sitting in a list slot or
 a map value is VAL-21's own claim and belongs to that leaf, not to a
 payload builder that no row asserts.)

To 'gen scalar element'.
    a number called tag is 'rng below' of 4.
    a text called payload is 'gen value payload' of tag.
    a text called 'the element' is 'gen value expression' of tag and payload.
    Return a text, 'the element'.

(A list payload. Every one used to be exactly `[number, "text"]` - two
 elements, that pair of types, that order - and no line of the manual
 asks for any of it. Count runs 0 to 4 (the empty list is reachable and
 keeps its tag, hand-verified) and each slot draws its own type.)

To 'gen list payload'.
    a number called 'how many elements' is 'rng below' of 5.
    a text called 'the elements' is "".
    a number called 'elements built' is 0.
    While 'elements built' is less than 'how many elements',
        a text called 'an element' is 'gen scalar element',
        If 'elements built' is 0 then,
            Set 'the elements' to 'an element'.
        If 'elements built' is greater than 0 then,
            Set 'the elements' to "{'the elements'}, {'an element'}".
        Set 'elements built' to 'elements built' add 1.
    a text called 'a list literal' is "[{'the elements'}]".
    Return a text, 'a list literal'.

(A map payload, on the same reasoning: one entry keyed by a text and
 holding a number was an invariant nobody declared. Count runs 0 to 3,
 the empty map included, and each value draws its own type. Two entries
 may draw the same key - legal, and nothing here asserts a map's length.)

To 'gen map payload'.
    a number called 'how many entries' is 'rng below' of 4.
    a text called 'the entries' is "".
    a number called 'entries built' is 0.
    While 'entries built' is less than 'how many entries',
        a text called 'a key' is 'gen word from' of 'the value nouns',
        a text called 'a mapped value' is 'gen scalar element',
        a text called 'an entry' is "\"{'a key'}\": {'a mapped value'}",
        If 'entries built' is 0 then,
            Set 'the entries' to 'an entry'.
        If 'entries built' is greater than 0 then,
            Set 'the entries' to "{'the entries'}, {'an entry'}".
        Set 'entries built' to 'entries built' add 1.
    a text called 'a map literal' is "{{{'the entries'}}}".
    Return a text, 'a map literal'.

To 'gen value payload' with a number called tag.
    If tag is 0 then,
        a text called 'a whole number' is 'gen extreme number',
        Return a text, 'a whole number'.
    If tag is 1 then,
        a text called 'some words' is 'gen text payload',
        Return a text, 'some words'.
    If tag is 2 then,
        a text called 'a decimal number' is 'gen extreme float',
        Return a text, 'a decimal number'.
    If tag is 3 then,
        a number called 'the truth' is 'rng below' of 2,
        If 'the truth' is 0 then,
            Return a text, "false".
        Return a text, "true".
    If tag is 4 then,
        a text called 'a list literal' is 'gen list payload',
        Return a text, 'a list literal'.
    If tag is 5 then,
        a text called 'a map literal' is 'gen map payload',
        Return a text, 'a map literal'.
    (LANGUAGE.md:2596 - all three spellings produce the identical value,
     so picking one of them always would be an undeclared rule.)
    a number called 'the absent spelling' is 'rng below' of 3.
    If 'the absent spelling' is 0 then,
        Return a text, "nothing".
    If 'the absent spelling' is 1 then,
        Return a text, "null".
    Return a text, "nil".

To 'gen value expression' with a number called tag and a text called payload.
    If tag is 1 then,
        a text called 'a text literal' is "\"{payload}\"",
        Return a text, 'a text literal'.
    Return a text, payload.

To 'gen value shown' with a number called tag and a text called payload.
    If tag is 1 then,
        a text called 'the escaped literal' is "\\\"{payload}\\\"",
        Return a text, 'the escaped literal'.
    Return a text, payload.

(What an assertion message may put after `got`. Interpolating a value
 whose tag is dynamic text, float, list or map prints the raw payload as
 an integer, so those report the tag instead; a number or a boolean
 interpolates faithfully and reports the payload.)

To 'gen got phrase' with a text called name and a text called possessive and a number called tag.
    If tag is 0 then,
        a text called 'the payload itself' is "{{{name}}}",
        Return a text, 'the payload itself'.
    If tag is 3 then,
        a text called 'the truth itself' is "{{{name}}}",
        Return a text, 'the truth itself'.
    a text called 'the tag instead' is 'gen type phrase' of possessive.
    Return a text, 'the tag instead'.

To 'gen type phrase' with a text called possessive.
    a text called 'the tag reading' is "{{{possessive} type}}".
    Return a text, 'the tag reading'.

To 'gen tag other than' with a number called 'the forbidden tag'.
    a number called 'a tag' is 'rng below' of 7.
    While 'a tag' is equal to 'the forbidden tag',
        Set 'a tag' to 'rng below' of 7.
    Return a number, 'a tag'.

(The same draw restricted to the six tags a predicate can name -
 `nothing` matches none of them (LANGUAGE.md:2600), so a leaf that has
 to ASSERT a tag cannot be handed one.)

To 'gen taggable other than' with a number called 'the forbidden tag'.
    a number called 'a tag' is 'rng below' of 6.
    While 'a tag' is equal to 'the forbidden tag',
        Set 'a tag' to 'rng below' of 6.
    Return a number, 'a tag'.

To 'gen tag list has' with a list called tags and a number called 'the wanted tag'.
    a boolean called 'the tag appears' is false.
    For each 'a tag' in tags,
        If 'a tag' is 'the wanted tag' then,
            Set 'the tag appears' to true..
    Return a boolean, 'the tag appears'.

(How a dispatch branch announces itself. The manual's own example prints
 a bare tag word, but nothing requires that wording, so the three styles
 are drawn per leaf.)

To 'gen branch label' with a number called style and a text called 'the tag name'.
    If style is 0 then,
        Return a text, 'the tag name'.
    If style is 1 then,
        a text called 'the sorted form' is "sorted: {'the tag name'}",
        Return a text, 'the sorted form'.
    a text called 'the found form' is "found {'the tag name'}".
    Return a text, 'the found form'.

(VAL-03 (LANGUAGE.md:2450-2460): a `value` parameter dispatches on
 `is a number` / `is a text` / `is a float` / `is a boolean` /
 `is a list` / `is a map` / Otherwise, and the tag it reads is the tag
 the caller passed.

 The wording of every assertion line in this file - `ASSERT VAL-NN:
 expected <x> got <y>`, then `Exit 95.` - is fixed by PROCEDURE.md §6,
 which reserves 95 for a ledger assertion and makes the runner carry
 that line as the finding's detail. So `ASSERT`, `expected`, `got` and
 `VAL` recurring in most programs is a DECLARED rule, not an undeclared
 one: it is the finding protocol, and this is its citation.

 The assertion lives in the CALLEE, which is the only place that can see
 what arrived. The generator picks one tag it will never pass and gives
 that tag a branch whose body exits 95: if the compiler ever routes an
 argument there, a tag was lost across the call. The closing Otherwise
 asserts the other half - that every argument reached SOME branch -
 except when a `nothing` was passed, which matches no predicate at all
 (there is no `is a nothing`, LANGUAGE.md:2600) and legitimately lands
 there.

 Branch order is rotated because nothing pins it: the six predicates are
 mutually exclusive, hand-verified, so any order classifies the same.)

To 'gen dispatch branch' with a text called indent and a text called 'the parameter phrase' and a number called tag and a boolean called 'was passed' and a boolean called 'is forbidden' and a number called 'branches so far' and a number called 'the label style'.
    a boolean called 'this branch is wanted' is 'was passed'.
    If 'is forbidden' then,
        Set 'this branch is wanted' to true.
    If 'this branch is wanted' is false then,
        Return a number, 'branches so far'.
    a text called opener is "Otherwise if".
    If 'branches so far' is 0 then,
        Set opener to "If".
    a number called 'branches after this one' is 'branches so far' add 1.
    a text called 'the parameter name' is 'gen written name' of 'the parameter phrase'.
    a text called 'the parameter possessive' is 'gen possessive name' of 'the parameter phrase'.
    a text called 'the predicate' is 'gen tag predicate' of tag.
    a text called 'the tag name' is 'gen tag name' of tag.
    If 'is forbidden' then,
        a text called 'the tag it reported' is 'gen type phrase' of 'the parameter possessive',
        a text called 'the bare tag name' is 'gen bare tag name' of tag,
        a text called 'the assertion line' is "{indent}    {opener} {'the parameter name'} is {'the predicate'} then, Print \"ASSERT VAL-03: expected no {'the bare tag name'} argument got {'the tag it reported'}\", Exit 95.\n",
        'gen emit' of 'the assertion line',
        Return a number, 'branches after this one'.
