(VAL-06 (LANGUAGE.md:2479-2482): `value` is recognized only where a type
 is expected, so `a value is 5.` declares an ordinary variable NAMED
 value. Once per program - a second declaration would collide - and the
 same permission is what lets 'gen value parameter name' hand the word
 to a parameter in the two leaves above.

 The payload is a number, a float or a boolean - never a text.
 `a value is "some words".` compiles and compares correctly but reports
 `Number (dynamic)` for its type and PRINTS ITS POINTER, so a leaf that
 emitted it would put an address on stdout. Hand-reduced; recorded as a
 discrepancy for the master rather than encoded as a reading of what the
 form ought to mean - the row's claim is about the NAME, and any of the
 three settled payloads makes that claim on its own. (The boolean form
 reports `Number (dynamic)` too, though it prints and compares
 correctly - the same mis-tagging, one more data point for that
 discrepancy, and harmless here because this row asserts the payload and
 never the tag.)

 Which of the three is drawn is unpinned, so it is drawn.)

